    "lawCode": "Add §11-0535-b, amd §71-0925, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5098\n\nSPONSOR: MARTINEZ\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the environmental conservation law, in\nrelation to designating certain species as vulnerable species and\nprohibiting the sale of articles made from such vulnerable species; and\nto require the department of environmental conservation to designate the\ngiraffe as a vulnerable species\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide additional protections to vulner-\nable species of wildlife.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill:\n\n* provides that the Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC) may\ndesignate species as vulnerable species if they meet the following\ncriteria:\no on the basis of credible scientific evidence, the species is subject\nto population loss, loss of range, risk of extinction, or other factors\nthat may cause the species to go extinct;\no the species is known to be in trade in New York state;\no the species is not protected as an endangered or threatened species on\nthe federal level; and\no the species is not protected under the federal Marine Mammal\nProtection Act;\n\n* prohibits the sale, or possession with intent to sell, of any article\nmade in whole or in part from the skin, hide, or other parts of any\nvulnerable species, except under license or permit from the department;\n\n* allows the DEC to issue licenses or permits for the sale or possession\nwith intent to sell of any article made from the skin, hide, or other\nparts of a vulnerable species, provided that:\no the article is part of a bona fide antique; or\no the article is for bona fide educational or scientific purposes, or to\nbe made part of a museum, provided that the article is not thereafter\nsold to any private party;\n\n* requires DEC to maintain a list of vulnerable species and to post such\nlist on the Department's website;\n\n* provides that a species shall not be a vulnerable species if it is\nprotected by any other provision of the Environmental Conservation Law,\nor if its taking is regulated by DEC pursuant to permit or license;\n\n* adds a new subdivision 17 to § 71-0925 of the Environmental Conserva-\ntion Law to provide a civil penalty for violations of § 11-0535-b;\n\n* requires DEC, within 180 days of enactment, to promulgate regulations\nthat, at a minimum, designate giraffes as a vulnerable species;\n\n* requires DEC, no later than January 1, 2021, to prepare a report\noutlining enforcement activities and recommendations regarding any\nnecessary changes.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn August of 2012, a report was issued that detailed the extent of the\ngiraffe trade in the United States. The report indicated that this trade\nwas having a negative effect on the giraffe population, which has\ndeclined by 4096 since about 1990. Despite attempts to list the giraffe\nas endangered under the federal Endangered Species Act, giraffes are not\nprotected by any federal or state laws, and trade in their parts is\nlegal. This bill gives the DEC commissioner the ability to protect\nspecies such as the giraffe by designating them as vulnerable species\nand prohibiting the sale of any article made in whole or in part of the\nskin, hide, or other parts of the species. This will provide an interme-\ndiate step prior to endangered species designation, and allow the state\nto protect those species that do not currently enjoy protections on the\nstate or federal level, but are at risk for such a listing, or even\nworse, extinction due to trade.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nUndetermined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
